    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The numbers are heavily contested: chroniclers give figures as high as 400,000 for the combatants, while 19th-century German historians estimated about 7,500 to 15,000 Christians and about 12,000 to 20,000 Turks.</li><li>The crusaders had no single commander. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-holy-roman-emperor">Sigismund</a>'s advice to let the Turkish vanguard be met by Wallachian foot soldiers was rejected by the French, who insisted on leading the charge.</li><li>After the defeat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> made no further move to stop the Turkish advance in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> until the 1440s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> fell in 1453.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The nearest thing to an eyewitness account was written by Johann Schiltberger, who was 16 when he saw the battle, was captured and spent 30 years enslaved by the Turks before he returned home and wrote it.</p></aside>
    </section>

