    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1389</span><span class="rf-text">After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kosovo">Battle of Kosovo</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> conquer most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1394</span><span class="rf-text">Pope Boniface IX proclaims a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against the Turks, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> begin to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">April 1396</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> sets out from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1396</span><span class="rf-text">The crusaders come within view of Nicopolis and begin the siege</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1396</span><span class="rf-text">The French charge, are met by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid</a>'s reserve of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sipahi">sipahis</a>, and the crusader army is routed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1396</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid</a> has the prisoners brought before him, and many are executed while the nobles are spared for ransom</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1397</span><span class="rf-text">A ransom of 200,000 gold florins is agreed, and the noble prisoners are released on 24 June</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1444</span><span class="rf-text">Hungarians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and Wallachians are defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-varna">Battle of Varna</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-nicopolis" data-v="b952ff08a7" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The armies take position</b> The French knights rode out with their backs to Nicopolis. The Hungarians, Hospitallers and Germans stayed behind them, while the Turks stood on the slope to the south with their reserve of sipahis out of sight on the plateau.</li><li><b>The French charge the Turkish front line</b> The French knights charged and crushed the untrained conscripts at the front of the Turkish army. The conscripts broke and the knights pressed on towards the trained infantry behind them.</li><li><b>The knights fight through the stakes</b> The knights came under heavy fire from archers and were held up by rows of sharpened stakes. Many were unhorsed or dismounted to pull the stakes up, and the Turkish infantry fled behind the sipahis.</li><li><b>The sipahis counter-attack on the plateau</b> The French knights reached the plateau expecting to find the Turks in flight and met the sipahis whom Bayezid had kept in reserve. Some knights fled down the slope, and the rest fought on as sipahis also swept down along the flanks.</li><li><b>Nevers is taken and the French yield</b> The Turks threatened to overwhelm Nevers, and his bodyguard threw themselves to the ground to plead for his life. The Turks took Nevers prisoner, and seeing this, the rest of the French yielded.</li><li><b>The Wallachians leave and the Serbs arrive</b> The Hungarians and their allies fought in confused combat on the plain, while the Transylvanians and Wallachians decided the day was lost and left the field. A reinforcement of Serbian knights then came to the Turks.</li><li><b>Sigismund's force is overwhelmed</b> Sigismund, the Master of the Hospitallers and the Germans fought to prevent the envelopment, with heavy losses on both sides. The force was overwhelmed; Sigismund and the Master escaped by boat, and the rest of his army surrendered.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-holy-roman-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sigismund-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Sigismund, Holy Roman Emperor" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-holy-roman-emperor">Sigismund, Holy Roman Emperor</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pisanello_024.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Formerly attributed to Pisanello, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>There were many minor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusades</a> in the 14th century, undertaken by individual kings or knights, and the most recent, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> in 1390, had failed. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kosovo">Battle of Kosovo</a> in 1389 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> had conquered most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and reduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> to the area around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, which they blockaded from 1394. In 1393 the Bulgarian tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-shishman">Ivan Shishman</a> had lost Nicopolis, his temporary capital, to the Ottomans, and his brother Ivan Stratsimir held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidin">Vidin</a> as an Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> had become the frontier between the two religions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> feared for its territories and its influence over the Adriatic, Ionian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> seas, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> feared an Ottoman monopoly of the trade routes to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, where it had colonies.</p><p>In 1394 Pope Boniface IX proclaimed a new crusade, although the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-schism">Western Schism</a> had split the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the days when a pope could call a crusade were long past. Two factors decided its formation: a truce in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> between Richard II's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and Charles VI's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold">Philip II, Duke of Burgundy</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>'s interest lay chiefly in the prestige and power of himself and his house, and Barbara Tuchman notes that opulent display became the dominant theme, with plans, logistics and intelligence about the enemy coming second, if at all. A Hungarian delegation then reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> to ask for help on King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-holy-roman-emperor">Sigismund</a>'s behalf, and French nobles responded with enthusiasm.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-duke-of-orleans"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-i-duke-of-orleans.jpg" alt="Louis I, Duke of Orléans" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-duke-of-orleans">Louis I, Duke of Orléans</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Attribu%C3%A9_%C3%A0_Colart_de_Laon_-_Louis_d%27Orl%C3%A9ans_(detail_de_la_Pri%C3%A8re_dans_les_jardins_des_oliviers).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Possibly Colart de Laon / Colart de Laon, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The number of combatants is heavily contested. Chroniclers give figures as high as 400,000, each side insisting that the enemy outnumbered it two to one. Tuchman dismisses the often-given figure of 100,000 crusaders, since so many men would have taken a month to cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-gates">Iron Gates</a> and the crusaders took eight days. Johann Schiltberger, who saw the battle at the age of 16, put the crusaders at 17,000 and the Turks at 200,000. German historians of the 19th century reached about 7,500 to 15,000 Christians and about 12,000 to 20,000 Turks, noting that the countryside could not have fed scores of thousands of men and horses.</p><p>About 5,000 French knights and squires were said to have joined, with 6,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and foot soldiers, some 11,000 men in all. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> came, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> supplied ships, and Hungarian envoys encouraged German princes to join. Reports of 3,000 English knights, taken as fact by some historians, are doubted by others, since no English financial or royal preparations are recorded; Hospitallers of the English langue may account for mentions of the English. The Ottoman army is also estimated at 15,000 to 20,000, though some sources give up to 60,000. It included 1,500 Serbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-lazarevic">Stefan Lazarević</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid</a>'s brother-in-law and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>.</p><p>Command was ambiguous. Philip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-gaunt">John of Gaunt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-duke-of-orleans">Louis of Orléans</a> withdrew, and Burgundy named his 24-year-old son John, Count of Nevers, to nominal command, with the experienced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enguerrand-vii-de-coucy">Enguerrand de Coucy</a> as chief counsellor. The French rules of March 1396 gave the Count and his company the vanguard.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a> on 30 April 1396 and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-holy-roman-emperor">Sigismund</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a>. Philip's son Nevers had lingered at German courts and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> only on 24 June. At a war council in Buda, Sigismund advised letting the Turks make the long march to the crusaders, but the French rejected this. The army marched down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, crossed at Orşova and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidin">Vidin</a>, whose ruler Stratsimir surrendered. At Oryahovo the inhabitants agreed to surrender to Sigismund, but the French broke the agreement, pillaging and massacring the town. On 12 September the crusaders reached Nicopolis. With no siege machines they settled down to starve the garrison out, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid</a> marched quickly to its relief. On the 24th Sigismund proposed that Wallachian foot soldiers meet the Turkish vanguard, but the Constable d'Eu rejected this as demeaning to the knights, and prisoners taken at Oryahovo were massacred.</p><p>On 25 September the French vanguard, under d'Eu, rode out and crushed the untrained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a> of the Turkish front line, though stakes hampered the horses. Coucy and Jean de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a> urged a pause, but the younger knights pursued up the hill, many on foot. On the plateau they met a fresh corps of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sipahi">sipahis</a> that Bayezid had kept in reserve. Vienne was killed, Nevers was taken prisoner, and the rest of the French yielded. Sipahis meanwhile swept down the flanks. The Transylvanians and Wallachians left the field, and the arrival of 1,500 Serbian knights proved critical against Sigismund's force. Sigismund and the Hospitaller Master escaped by boat to the Venetian ships.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-holy-roman-emperor">Sigismund</a> later said to the Hospitaller Master, "We lost the day by the pride and vanity of these French." On 26 September <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid</a> had the prisoners assembled. Jacques de Helly identified the chief nobles for ransom, among them Nevers, Coucy, Bar, d'Eu and La Tremoïlle, and those judged under 20 were taken as slaves. The rest, thought to number several thousand, were bound in groups and killed by decapitation or by the severing of limbs, while Nevers and the nobles watched. The killing lasted from early morning until late afternoon, when Bayezid called it off. The number of dead in the executions is said to have ranged from 300 to 3,000, and the number dead on the battlefield was much greater.</p><p>Few of those who fled survived. Men drowned swimming for the boats or the far bank, and those on board pushed away others trying to climb in. Sigismund sailed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and went home by sea. Survivors who crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> found the land already stripped by the Wallachians, and many starved. The captives were marched about 350 miles to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a> and then taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bursa">Bursa</a>. News reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in December, and Jacques de Helly rode in on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a>. A ransom of 200,000 gold florins was agreed in June 1397. Coucy, d'Eu and others died in captivity or soon after, and the last of the leaders re-entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in February 1398. A three-sided transaction between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, Sigismund and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> took 27 years to settle.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Nicopolis&#8221; cites where it covers Captives and ransom, Broader ramifications:</p><ul><li>Barbara W. Tuchman, A Distant Mirror: the Calamitous 14th Century (Alfred A. Knopf, 1978)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jean-froissart"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-froissart.jpg" alt="Jean Froissart" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jean-froissart">Jean Froissart</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean-Froissart.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacques Le Boucq, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The closest record to a first-person account is by Johann Schiltberger, a German in the following of a Bavarian noble, who saw the battle at 16, was captured and was enslaved by the Turks for 30 years. He wrote a narrative after his return, but his figure for the Turks, 200,000, is wildly inflated. The Chronica Hungarorum, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-froissart">Jean Froissart</a>, the Monk of St Denis and the poet Deschamps tell of the king's mood and of the grief in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and the Ottoman historian Şükrullah, writing in the 1460s, gave 130,000 crusaders and 60,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. Medieval chroniclers had reasons to exaggerate: each side's numbers could comfort the losers or magnify the winners' glory.</p><p>Much is disputed. Tuchman writes that out of the welter of versions a coherent account of the movements on the field is not to be had. Chroniclers blamed the defeat on the crusaders' immorality, but Tuchman cautions that this was a contemporary tendency and cannot be verified. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-galeazzo-visconti">Gian Galeazzo Visconti</a> was widely believed to have given <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid</a> intelligence, though there is no firm evidence and he may have been a scapegoat. The English presence is doubted by some historians.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/crusade-of-varna"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/crusade-of-varna.jpg" alt="Crusade of Varna" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/crusade-of-varna">Crusade of Varna</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Varna_1444.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jan Matejko, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Nicopolis is often called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusade</a> of Nicopolis, one of the last major crusades of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade-of-varna">Crusade of Varna</a> in 1443 to 1444. By their victory the Turks discouraged the formation of European coalitions against them. They maintained their pressure on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, tightened their control over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and became a greater threat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>. After the defeat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> made no further moves to stop the Turkish advance in the Balkans until the 1440s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> soon renewed their war.</p><p>Wallachia continued its stance against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, repelling expeditions in 1397 and 1400. In 1402 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid</a> was defeated and captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>, and Mircea and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> took advantage of the anarchy to campaign against the Ottomans. The Hungarians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and Wallachians were defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-varna">Battle of Varna</a> in 1444, and Constantinople fell in 1453, followed by the Despotate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> in 1460, the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> in 1461 and the Principality of Theodoro in 1475. The battle is also widely regarded as the end of the Second Bulgarian Empire, since hopes for its revival had come to an end, and its last ruler, Ivan Stratsimir, was captured and killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bursa">Bursa</a>.</p><p>Johan Huizinga remarked, with a historian's hindsight, on the lamentable consequences of statecraft recklessly embarking on an enterprise of vital import in the spirit of a chivalrous adventure, though participants and contemporary chroniclers did not analyse the event in these terms.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Nicopolis on 25 September 1396 a crusader army led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-holy-roman-emperor">Sigismund</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and a French contingent was routed by the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a>, who then had many prisoners executed. The defeat ended the Second Bulgarian Empire and discouraged European coalitions against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</em></p>

